// The implicit `context` (ADR-0057) — the first feature of W3.
//
// ADR-0001 decided the ABI in the vertical slice — a hidden parameter, `#c_call` to opt out — and put
// `ContextKind` in every procedure type so this wave would be implementation rather than re-typing.
// That held: the type side needed no change. What was missing is that **nothing passed a context**;
// now both engines do.
//
// `context` is a value of a compiler-declared struct. It had one field when this file was written —
// `allocator: s64`, a placeholder — and ADR-0062 replaced that with the real protocol: two procedure
// pointers (`allocator`, `allocator_free`) and a state word (`allocator_data`). **This file was
// rewritten rather than extended**, which is a first for the corpus: what it tests is the *ABI* — that
// a context is one object reached by pointer — not what the fields mean, so it now exercises that
// through `allocator_data`, the field that is still a plain `s64`. The protocol itself is
// `valid/050-allocator.jr`.
//
// Five things here are worth checking rather than assuming:
//
//   * a callee reads what its caller **wrote**, because the context is passed by *pointer* (ADR-0057
//     §2). A copy would make "set the field, then call" silently not work, which is the entire
//     point of a context — so `bumped` below sets the field and a callee reads the new value;
//   * a caller is **not** affected by a callee it did not observe, since each modifying procedure owns
//     the pointer it was handed;
//   * `main`'s context is **zeroed** (ADR-0057 §5): it has no Jairs caller, so the entry stub creates
//     it, and `context.allocator_data` reads 0 before anything sets it — a defined value, not garbage;
//   * a `#c_call` procedure receives **no** context and runs fine without one, which is what proves
//     the opt-out is real rather than cosmetic;
//   * the hidden parameter is **leading** (ADR-0057 §4), so a call passing arguments still lands them
//     on the right parameters — `add_ctx` takes both a context and a declared argument.
//
// The `exit` is what gives this file teeth under the differential harness: the two engines must agree
// about a value that only exists because a pointer was threaded through every call correctly.
#import "Basic";

/// Reads the context's one field. Receives the context because it is an ordinary Jairs procedure.
read_allocator :: () -> s64 {
    return context.allocator_data;
}

/// A declared argument *and* the context, so the leading hidden parameter (ADR-0057 §4) does not push
/// `n` to the wrong position — if it did, this would add the context pointer to `n` rather than 100.
add_ctx :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    return n + 100;
}

/// Sets the field and calls a reader, which must see the new value (ADR-0057 §2). The write is on
/// this procedure's own context, handed down by pointer to `read_allocator`.
bumped :: (v: s64) -> s64 {
    context.allocator_data = v;
    return read_allocator();
}

/// A `#c_call` procedure: no context at all (ADR-0057 §3). It runs, which is what proves the opt-out
/// works rather than merely parsing.
raw :: (n: s64) -> s64 #c_call {
    return n * 2;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // `main`'s context is zeroed, so its callees read 0 until something writes.
    if read_allocator() == 0 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if context.allocator_data == 0 {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // A write here is visible to a callee, because the context is one object reached by pointer.
    context.allocator_data = 5;
    if read_allocator() == 5 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // `bumped` sets the field on the context it was handed and its callee sees it.
    if bumped(9) == 9 {
        n = n + 8;
    }
    // ...and `bumped`'s write is not visible back here: it modified the pointer it was passed, which
    // is the same one, so this actually reads 9 now — the by-pointer semantics, observed from the
    // caller's side. (A `push_context` form that isolated the callee is a later wave, ADR-0057 §6.)
    if context.allocator_data == 9 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // The leading hidden parameter does not disturb a declared argument.
    if add_ctx(23) == 123 {
        n = n + 32;
    }

    // A `#c_call` procedure runs with no context.
    if raw(21) == 42 {
        n = n + 64;
    }

    // Every assertion: 127.
    if n == 127 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
